Z||000000||FIL||10-20-2001||11:40 AM||||If I would remove my original Catalytics from my 87GT and removed the insides <BR>of them (drill them out), and reinstall an "empty" catalytic converter, <BR>should I expect an increase in Horsepower and if so how much?  Would you <BR>recommend this?  Just a couple of questions I have.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-20-2001||05:35 PM||||No and No||||reg||1
Z||000002||RacinRob||10-20-2001||05:37 PM||||I always heard it as yes an no<BR>you might gain 2 to 3 HP and lose 3 to 4 FLB TQ <BR>by anay means it is probably not worth it||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||10-20-2001||07:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FIL:<BR><B>If I would remove my original Catalytics from my 87GT and removed the insides <BR>of them (drill them out), and reinstall an "empty" catalytic converter, <BR>should I expect an increase in Horsepower and if so how much?  Would you <BR>recommend this?  Just a couple of questions I have.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey FIL!<BR>If you need to keep the shell to pass inspection, try to run a pipe through the shell...<BR>If you don't need it for inspection, then run a straight pipe...<BR>The problem with running a gutted shell is turbulance causing backpressure...<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||10-20-2001||07:46 PM||||Well, you do need to either run a pipe through the center or just put on a straight pipe to keep the turbulence from killing your exhaust flow.  You can expect anywhere from 2-5HP increase at high rpms, with a slight 2 or 3 HP decrease down low.  The car will rev easier, too.  It'll also be louder.  I ran a straight pipe on my Formula for a while.  I had to put another cat back on for emissions, and I was getting tired of the sound.  Sounded great at idle, but didn't have a good sound wide open.  Not too loud, just not a well tuned sound.  I've heard putting a glasspack in place of the cat will help with that.  Experiment and see what you like.  Don't expect a real noticeable difference in power, though.  When I put a new hi-flo cat back on, I didn't notice much, if any, decrease in performance.  ||||reg||1
